From 14d1a650768ad19a862997f8fad5071fc663feb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:51:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] The chroma of 4:2:0 video where it is,
 with Lanczos 3, a level down where the GPU does not keep up

Kodi's YUV shaders of GLES read the chroma planes of a 4:2:0 picture with the
texture's bilinear filter at the place of each output pixel, and take every
video's chroma samples as centred between two luma samples. Most video has
them elsewhere: MPEG-2, H.264 and HEVC on the left, co-sited with the first
luma sample of each pair, and UHD Blu-ray top-left. So every SDR, HDR10 and
HLG video that Kodi draws itself (software decoding, VAAPI with its planes
imported one by one, NVIDIA through nvidia-vaapi-driver) had its colour a
quarter of a chroma sample off, and bilinear between samples. The bridge
already draws its pictures with Lanczos 3 where the video says.

Measured with these very shaders on the software GPU, against 4:4:4
references made from 4K and 1080p masters (the luma reduced to where the
chroma samples are), Delta E ITP (BT.2124):
- the samples where the video places them, with the same bilinear filter (as
  cheap as before): -6 % on average for animation, -21 % for film in PQ,
  -19 % in SDR;
- Lanczos 3 over 6 x 6 samples: -13 %, -26 %, -24 %; its 99th percentile
  -18 %, -26 %, -26 %;
- Spline16 over 4 x 4: -12 %, -25 %, -23 %, with less than half the samples.
libplacebo measures the same filters within 2 %.

gles_yuv2rgb_chroma.frag takes the chroma at the video's siting
(m_chromaShift, from VideoPicture::chroma_position; an unspecified one as
left, as libplacebo takes it), read at the centres of the texels it weighs,
with the chroma textures read GL_NEAREST for the filters of more than one
sample (a bilinear filter of fixed-point weights may weigh a neighbour in
where a centre falls a hair short of it) and the samplers highp (a lowp
sample may be a 16-bit float, as on Mesa's llvmpipe). It is inserted
before main() of the basic shader and of the filter shader of GLES 3.1, for
the 4:2:0 formats only (NV12, NV12_RRG, and the planar formats of 4:2:0: the
same shader formats carry 4:2:2 and 4:4:4, which keep Kodi's way, as do the
packed formats and the bob shader of interlaced fields). Without its defines
the shaders draw the same bytes as before.

Each video starts at Lanczos 3. Its pictures are timed on the GPU
(GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query) and the filter goes a level down, to Spline16
and then to bilinear at the video's siting, where they do not keep up: two
windows of 48 pictures in a row in which 5 % of them, 3 at least, take more
than 80 % of a refresh, or one whose pictures take more than the refresh on
average (the marks of the step-down of native Dolby Vision for frames that
wait). The first 48 pictures after a start or a change are left out; a level
is not taken back up within a video. kodi.log says how each video's chroma is
drawn, what its pictures took, and each step. A shader that a GPU's compiler
does not take leaves the video at bilinear at its siting, never without a
picture. The scaling method Nearest keeps the sample nearest to each pixel,
now where the video places it. advancedsettings.xml can force a level for every video, to compare
them or to measure what each costs: <video><chromastep>N</chromastep>, 0
Lanczos 3, 1 Spline16, 2 bilinear at the video's siting, 3 Kodi's own way.
